# Secrets - never commit. verbatra loads .env automatically and reads the
# provider key (GEMINI_API_KEY) from the environment only. The root .gitignore
# already ignores these; this local copy makes the boundary explicit and mirrors
# what `verbatra init` scaffolds for any consuming project.
.env
.env.local

# ...but the template (key name only, no real value) is meant to be committed.
!.env.example

# verbatra's local-only run-status snapshot (review flags, token usage); never committed.
.verbatra-local/
verbatra.cache.json

# Generated from packages/cli/package.json and packages/studio/package.json by
# scripts/sync-version.mjs, which dev, build and typecheck all run first. Tracking it meant every local docs run dirtied the working tree and the
# committed copy drifted behind the published version (it sat three releases stale), sweeping
# resync noise into unrelated pull requests. Nothing user-facing depended on the committed value:
# the sync runs before `next build`, so the deployed site always showed the correct version.
lib/version.generated.json

# next dev regenerates these on every run (Next.js 16's agent-guidance files,
# see node_modules/next/dist/server/lib/generate-agent-files.js). This app has
# its own CLAUDE.md content at the repo root; the generated copy here is
# Next.js's own dev-server noise, not project content, so it is not committed.
AGENTS.md
CLAUDE.md

next-env.d.ts

# Generated from the git history of content/docs by scripts/sync-translation-freshness.mjs, which
# dev, build, typecheck and test all run first; it drives the outdated-translation notice.
lib/translation-freshness.generated.json
